lesson:
**Polymorphism** means "many shapes": one variable type, many real kinds of object behind it, each doing its own thing when you call the same method.

```
List<Enemy> wave = List.of(new Slime(), new Archer(), new Boss(2));
int total = 0;
for (Enemy e : wave) total += e.attack();
```

The loop only knows `Enemy`. But when it calls `e.attack()`, Java looks at the *real* object at run time and runs Slime's `attack`, then Archer's, then Boss's. This is called **dynamic dispatch**. The variable's type (Enemy) decides *what you're allowed to call*; the object's real class decides *which code runs*.

Why it matters: without it, you'd write long chains of "if it's a slime do this, if it's an archer do that" everywhere: in the attack code, the reward code, the drawing code, the save code. Add a new enemy and you must find and fix every chain. With polymorphism you add one class and every loop just works. Games are built on this: the main loop calls `update()` and `draw()` on hundreds of different objects.

**instanceof and pattern matching.** Sometimes you really do need to ask what something is: loot dropped in a dungeon could be potions, weapons or coins, held as `List<Object>`, or you're handling objects from someone else's code. Modern Java checks and casts in one step:

```
if (item instanceof Potion p) {
    heal += p.heal();          // p is already a Potion
} else if (item instanceof Coin c) {
    gold += c.value();
}
```

`instanceof` is `false` for `null`, so it's also a safe null check.

The habit: **if you control the classes, prefer a method on each class** (polymorphism). Use `instanceof` when the types are mixed and you can't, or shouldn't, add a method to them. If you find yourself writing `instanceof` chains about your own classes, that's a sign the behaviour belongs inside the classes.

A subtle point: dispatch happens on **overridden** methods only. Fields and static methods don't dispatch. And overloading (same name, different parameters) is picked at compile time from the variable's type, not the real object.

Real uses: a payroll loop over nurses, doctors and volunteers (each knows its own pay); a robot's list of `Sensor`s each reading in its own way; a drawing program's list of shapes.

example.code:
import java.util.List;

abstract class Unit {
    abstract String act();
}

class Farmer extends Unit { String act() { return "plants maize"; } }
class Trader extends Unit { String act() { return "sells beans"; } }
class Vet extends Unit    { String act() { return "checks the cows"; } }

public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Unit> village = List.of(new Farmer(), new Trader(), new Vet());
        for (Unit u : village) System.out.println(u.act());   // dynamic dispatch

        Object[] things = { "rain", 42, 3.5, new Vet() };
        for (Object o : things) {
            if (o instanceof String s) System.out.println("text of length " + s.length());
            else if (o instanceof Integer n) System.out.println("whole number " + (n + 1));
            else if (o instanceof Unit u) System.out.println("a unit that " + u.act());
            else System.out.println("something else: " + o);
        }
    }
}

